The marked load reads sequentially (%rsi increases by 8 bytes each iteration), so it touches consecutive cache lines in ascending order, which is exactly the pattern a nextline prefetcher handles. Degree 2 keeps the prefetches 2 lines ahead, giving the in-order core enough latency hiding (L2/DRAM) without over-fetching past the end of the region and evicting useful data. Stride would also work but nextline is simpler and equally accurate here; stream's confirmation overhead and degree-4 nextline's extra useless prefetches make them worse choices. 

{"prefetcher": "nextline", "degree": 2}

The load advances sequentially by 8 bytes each inner-loop iteration (contiguous ascending addresses within a row), so its access pattern is a simple unit-stride stream where each 64-byte line is consumed fully before the next.

A next-line prefetcher of degree 1 covers this perfectly: one line ahead is fetched while the current line's 8 doubles are being consumed (~enough time to hide the 14-cycle L2 latency), and no useless prefetches are issued to pollute the cache. Stride would also work but adds detection overhead; higher degrees would prefetch lines not needed for many iterations, risking eviction of live data in the 32 KB L1.

{"prefetcher": "nextline", "degree": 1}

The marked load accesses `0x50(%rax)` where `%rax` advances by `%rsi` (one 128-byte row stride, i.e. 2 cache lines) each inner-loop iteration, so it touches exactly one line per iteration with a constant stride of 2 lines — a perfect match for the stride prefetcher.

Degree 2 fetches two strides ahead (≈ 14+ cycle L2 latency needs lead time), and since every prefetched line is subsequently consumed by this same instruction, there is no pollution.

